"""Leading with AI (Leadership Edition), Session 10: AI agents: delegating work that acts.  Written 12 November 2026, on the pattern of the pilots.

Pattern: recall and a hook with a real case; the idea as a ladder of pictures with no maths (house-sitter, chatbot plus tools plus loop, the loop,
four kinds of key, levels of freedom, least privilege, human approval, log and off switch, hidden instructions, good and bad at); the six-industry
tour and three case stories; recorded demonstrations; your people; questions to ask; the portfolio hands-on; the decision; the take-away.

Cases (research/*.md): ENR-05 Octopus Energy Arlo (hook), GAP-19 Centenary Group and Huawei (tour), TEL-05 Airtel spam alert (tour; added because
the plan has no telecom case of AI that acts), AGR-13 John Deere See & Spray (tour; added for the same reason in agriculture), MFG-11 Heidelberg
Materials kiln (tour and case), HLT-14 Lemonade (tour and the owner slide), PUB-03 SARS (tour and case), PUB-12 Robodebt (failure case),
BNK-07 Capitec and RTL-05 Jumia (your people), ADO-04 Gartner agent cancellation forecast (warning slide).
Demonstration: the recorded Case Runs of the Executive Edition, F4 (hidden instruction, two runs) and F7 (reply drafts, run 1), Claude, fresh
sessions given only the prompt, 4 November 2026 (Applied AI and Automation - Executive Edition/05 Course Maintenance/Case Runs/runs/).
The mailbox agent run is NOT recorded (demo_runs/s11_mailbox_agent.md: platform safeguards stopped every attempt), so no agent output from it is
shown; it may be run live, with the lecturer's key (written before any run) as the fallback.
The overdue-invoice example and the log lines are illustrations for Nile Valley Foods, the fictional course company.
"""


