    word_icons=['robot', 'gear', 'switch', 'lock', 'person', 'flag'],
    words_note='Six words for today. An agent is a chatbot with tools and a loop. A tool is an action a program carries out for it. The autonomy level, its level of freedom, says how much it does before asking. Least privilege means only the keys the task needs. Human-in-the-loop means a person approves before an action runs. And prompt injection, from Session 9, is a hidden instruction. We begin where Session 9 left off.',
    cover_note='Welcome back. In Session 9 we saw how AI goes wrong: it invents, misses the right source, repeats bias, obeys hidden instructions and drifts, and we built a routine to check it. Until now a person always stood between the AI’s answer and the world. Today that changes, because an agent can act: send the email, pay the supplier, adjust the machine. Before switching an agent on, you have to answer one question: what may an AI agent do on its own, and who approves? We start with an energy company, build the idea from drawings, tour six industries, watch two recorded runs, and then set a level of freedom for each entry in your portfolio.',
    outcome_note='Three things today. You will describe an agent as a chatbot with tools and a loop, and say which of its actions cannot be undone. You will hold the controls: the level of freedom, only the keys it needs, human approval, the log, the owner and the off switch. And your portfolio will carry a level of freedom for every entry, with one Tool Boundary Sheet filled.',
    keep_note='Keep the take-away sheet. It has the agent loop, the four kinds of key, the five levels of freedom, the controls to write down before any agent starts, the three signs of a hidden instruction, the six questions, and today’s recorded result: two runs refused the hidden order and made no tool call, and the drafts kept the rules. Your portfolio now carries a level of freedom for every entry. Session 11 is our first practical: we redesign one workflow step by step and work out what it is worth, so bring one task your team repeats every week.',
